## sn46.38
sn46.38:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, sometimes a noble disciple pays attention, applies the mind, concentrates wholeheartedly, and actively listens to the teaching. At such a time the five hindrances are absent, and the seven awakening factors are fully developed. | T: “Monks, whenever a disciple of the noble ones listens to the Dhamma—receptive, attentive, focusing his entire awareness, lending ear—on that occasion his five hindrances don’t exist, and seven factors for awakening go to the completion of their development.
sn46.38:2.1 | S: What are the five hindrances that are absent? | T: “And which of his five hindrances don’t exist on that occasion?
sn46.38:2.2 | S: Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. | T: The hindrance of sensual desire doesn’t exist on that occasion. The hindrance of ill will… The hindrance of sloth & drowsiness… The hindrance of restlessness & anxiety… The hindrance of uncertainty doesn’t exist on that occasion.
sn46.38:2.3 | S: These are the five hindrances that are absent. | T: “His five hindrances don’t exist on that occasion.
sn46.38:3.1 | S: And what are the seven awakening factors that are fully developed? | T: “And which seven factors for awakening go to the completion of their development on that occasion?
sn46.38:3.2 | S: The awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: Mindfulness as a factor for awakening goes to the completion of its development on that occasion. Analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening… Persistence as a factor for awakening… Rapture as a factor for awakening… Calm as a factor for awakening… Concentration as a factor for awakening… Equanimity as a factor for awakening goes to the completion of its development on that occasion.
sn46.38:3.3 | S: These are the seven awakening factors that are fully developed. | T: These are the seven factors for awakening that go to the completion of their development on that occasion.
sn46.38:3.4 | S: Sometimes a noble disciple pays attention, applies the mind, concentrates wholeheartedly, and actively listens to the teaching. At such a time the five hindrances are absent, and the seven awakening factors are fully developed.” | T: “Monks, whenever a disciple of the noble ones listens to the Dhamma—receptive, attentive, focusing his entire awareness, lending ear—on that occasion his five hindrances don’t exist, and these seven factors for awakening go to the completion of their development.”
sn46.38:3.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.4
sn46.4:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Sāriputta was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn46.4:1.2 | S: There Sāriputta addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
sn46.4:1.3 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: “Friend monks!”
sn46.4:1.4 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him.
sn46.4:1.5 | S: Sāriputta said this: | T: Ven. Sāriputta said,
sn46.4:2.1 | S: “There are these seven awakening factors. | T: “Friends, there are these seven factors for awakening.
sn46.4:2.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
sn46.4:2.3 | S: The awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: Mindfulness as a factor for awakening, analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, persistence as a factor for awakening, rapture as a factor for awakening, calm as a factor for awakening, concentration as a factor for awakening, & equanimity as a factor for awakening.
sn46.4:2.4 | S: These are the seven awakening factors. | T: These are the seven factors for awakening.
sn46.4:2.5 | S: In the morning, I meditate on whichever of these seven awakening factors I want. | T: “Whichever factor for awakening among these seven factors for awakening I want to dwell in during the morning, I dwell in that factor for awakening during the morning.
sn46.4:2.6 | S: At midday, | T: Whichever factor for awakening I want to dwell in during the middle of the day, I dwell in that factor for awakening during the middle of the day.
sn46.4:2.7 | S: and in the evening, I meditate on whichever of these seven awakening factors I want. | T: Whichever factor for awakening I want to dwell in during the late afternoon, I dwell in that factor for awakening during the late afternoon.
sn46.4:2.8 | S: If it’s the awakening factor of mindfulness, I know that it’s limitless and that it’s properly implemented. While it remains I understand that it remains. | T: “If the thought occurs to me, ‘mindfulness as a factor for awakening,’ the thought occurs to me, ‘It is immeasurable’; the thought occurs to me, ‘It is well-mastered.’ While it remains, I discern, ‘It remains.’
sn46.4:2.9 | S: And if it subsides in me I understand the specific reason it subsides. … | T: If it falls away from me, I discern, ‘It has fallen away from me because of this condition.’
sn46.4:2.10 | S: If it’s the awakening factor of equanimity, I know that it’s limitless and that it’s properly implemented. While it remains I understand that it remains. | T: [Similarly with analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, persistence as a factor for awakening, rapture as a factor for awakening, calm as a factor for awakening, and concentration as a factor for awakening.] “If the thought occurs to me, ‘equanimity as a factor for awakening,’ the thought occurs to me, ‘It is immeasurable’; the thought occurs to me, ‘It is well-mastered.’ While it remains, I discern, ‘It remains.’
sn46.4:2.11 | S: And if it subsides I understand the specific reason it subsides. | T: If it falls away from me, I discern, ‘It has fallen away from me because of this condition.’
