## sn46.52
sn46.52:1.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, early in the morning, a large number of monks adjusted their lower robes and, taking their bowls & outer robes, went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn46.52:1.2 | S: Then it occurred to him, | T: Then the thought occurred to them,
sn46.52:1.3 | S: “It’s too early to wander for alms in Sāvatthī. | T: “It’s still too early to go for alms in Sāvatthī.
sn46.52:1.4 | S: Why don’t we visit the monastery for the wanderers of other religions?” | T: Why don’t we go to the park of the wanderers of other sects?”
sn46.52:2.1 | S: Then they went to the monastery for the wanderers of other religions and exchanged greetings with the wanderers there. | T: So the monks went to the park of the wanderers of other sects. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with the wanderers of other sects.
sn46.52:2.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side. The wanderers said to them: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, the wanderers of other sects said to them,
sn46.52:3.1 | S: “Reverends, the ascetic Gotama teaches his disciples like this: | T: “Friends, Gotama the contemplative teaches the Dhamma to his disciples in this way:
sn46.52:3.2 | S: ‘Mendicants, please give up the five hindrances—corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom—and truly develop the seven awakening factors.’ | T: ‘Come, monks—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—develop the seven factors for awakening as they have come to be.’
sn46.52:3.3 | S: We too teach our disciples: | T: “Now, friends, we too teach our disciples in this way:
sn46.52:3.4 | S: ‘Reverends, please give up the five hindrances—corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom—and truly develop the seven awakening factors.’ | T: ‘Come, you friends—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—develop the seven factors for awakening as they have come to be.’
sn46.52:3.5 | S: What, then, is the difference between the ascetic Gotama’s teaching and instruction and ours?” | T: “So, friends, what difference, what distinction, what distinguishing factor is there here between Gotama the contemplative and us, when comparing Dhamma teaching with Dhamma teaching, instruction with instruction?”
sn46.52:4.1 | S: Those mendicants neither approved nor rejected that statement of the wanderers of other religions. | T: Then the monks neither delighted in the words of the wanderers of other sects, nor did they reject them.
sn46.52:4.2 | S: They rose from their seats, thinking: | T: Without delighting or rejecting, they got up from their seats and left, (thinking,)
sn46.52:4.3 | S: “We will learn the meaning of this statement from the Buddha himself.” | T: “We will learn the meaning of these words in the presence of the Blessed One.”
sn46.52:4.4 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: So, having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, the monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they [told him what had happened].
