sn46.52:8.5 | S: Because they’re out of their element. | T: Because it lies beyond their range.
sn46.52:8.6 | S: I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who could provide a satisfying answer to these questions except the Realized One or his disciple or someone who has heard it from them. | T: Monks, I don’t see anyone in this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, who would satisfy the mind with their answer to these questions, aside from the Tathāgata, a disciple of the Tathāgata, or one who had heard it from them.
sn46.52:9.1 | S: And what is the way in which the five hindrances become ten? | T: “And which, monks, is the exposition, following which, the five hindrances become ten?
sn46.52:9.2 | S: Sensual desire for what is internal is a hindrance; and sensual desire for what is external is also a hindrance. | T: “Any sensual desire for what is internal is a hindrance. Any sensual desire for what is external is a hindrance.
sn46.52:9.3 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of sensual desire’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘the hindrance of sensual desire’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:9.4 | S: Ill will for what is internal is a hindrance; and ill will for what is external is also a hindrance. | T: “Any ill will for what is internal is a hindrance. Any ill will for what is external is a hindrance.
sn46.52:9.5 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of ill will’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘the hindrance of ill will’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:9.6 | S: Dullness is a hindrance; and drowsiness is also a hindrance. | T: “Any sloth is a hindrance. Any drowsiness is a hindrance.
sn46.52:9.7 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of dullness and drowsiness’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘the hindrance of sloth & drowsiness’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:9.8 | S: Restlessness is a hindrance; and remorse is also a hindrance. | T: “Any restlessness is a hindrance. Any anxiety is a hindrance.
sn46.52:9.9 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of restlessness and remorse’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘the hindrance of restlessness & anxiety’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:9.10 | S: Doubt about internal things is a hindrance; and doubt about external things is also a hindrance. | T: “Any uncertainty over what is internal is a hindrance. Any uncertainty over what is external is a hindrance.
sn46.52:9.11 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of doubt’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘the hindrance of uncertainty’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:9.12 | S: This is the way in which the five hindrances become ten. | T: “This, monks, is the exposition, following which, the five hindrances become ten.
sn46.52:10.1 | S: And what is the way in which the seven awakening factors become fourteen? | T: “And which is the exposition, following which, the seven factors for awakening become fourteen?
sn46.52:10.2 | S: Mindfulness of internal things is the awakening factor of mindfulness; and mindfulness of external things is also the awakening factor of mindfulness. | T: “Any mindfulness concerning internal qualities is mindfulness as a factor for awakening. Any mindfulness concerning external qualities is mindfulness as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:10.3 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of mindfulness’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘mindfulness as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:11.1 | S: Investigating, exploring, and inquiring into internal things with wisdom is the awakening factor of investigation of principles; and investigating, exploring, and inquiring into external things with wisdom is also the awakening factor of investigation of principles. | T: “Whenever one, with discernment, investigates, carefully attends to, and makes an examination of internal qualities, that is analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening. Whenever one, with discernment, investigates, carefully attends to, and makes an examination of external qualities, that is analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:11.2 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of investigation of principles’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:12.1 | S: Physical energy is the awakening factor of energy; and mental energy is also the awakening factor of energy. | T: “Any bodily persistence is persistence as a factor for awakening. Any mental persistence is persistence as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:12.2 | S: That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of energy’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘persistence as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:13.1 | S: Rapture while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of rapture; and rapture without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of rapture. | T: “Any rapture accompanied by directed thought & evaluation is rapture as a factor for awakening. Any rapture unaccompanied by directed thought & evaluation is rapture as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:13.2 | S: In this way what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of rapture’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘rapture as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:14.1 | S: Physical tranquility is the awakening factor of tranquility; and mental tranquility is also the awakening factor of tranquility. | T: “Any bodily calm is calm as a factor for awakening. Any mental calm is calm as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:14.2 | S: In this way what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of tranquility’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘calm as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:15.1 | S: Immersion while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of immersion; and immersion without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of immersion. | T: “Any concentration accompanied by directed thought & evaluation is concentration as a factor for awakening. Any concentration unaccompanied by directed thought & evaluation is concentration as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:15.2 | S: In this way what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of immersion’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘concentration as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:16.1 | S: Equanimity for internal things is the awakening factor of equanimity; and equanimity for external things is also the awakening factor of equanimity. | T: “Any equanimity concerning internal qualities is equanimity as a factor for awakening. Any equanimity concerning external qualities is equanimity as a factor for awakening.
sn46.52:16.2 | S: In this way what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of equanimity’ becomes twofold. | T: Thus what comes under the heading of ‘equanimity as a factor for awakening’ becomes, by means of this exposition, twofold.
sn46.52:16.3 | S: This is the way in which the seven awakening factors become fourteen.” | T: “This, monks, is the exposition, following which, the seven factors for awakening are fourteen.”
sn46.52:16.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.53
sn46.53:1.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, early in the morning, a large number of monks adjusted their lower robes and, taking their bowls & outer robes, went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn46.53:1.2 | S: (The same as the previous discourse.) | T: Then the thought occurred to them, “It’s still too early to go for alms in Sāvatthī. Why don’t we go to the park of the wanderers of other sects?” So the monks went to the park of the wanderers of other sects. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with the wanderers of other sects. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, the wanderers of other sects said to them, “Friends, Gotama the contemplative teaches the Dhamma to his disciples in this way: ‘Come, monks—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—develop the seven factors for awakening as they have come to be.’ “Now, friends, we too teach our disciples in this way: ‘Come, you friends—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—develop the seven factors for awakening as they have come to be.’ “So, friends, what difference, what distinction, what distinguishing factor is there here between Gotama the contemplative and us, when comparing Dhamma teaching with Dhamma teaching, instruction with instruction?” Then the monks neither delighted in the words of the wanderers of other sects, nor did they reject them. Without delighting or rejecting, they got up from their seats and left, (thinking,) “We will learn the meaning of these words in the presence of the Blessed One.” So, having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, the monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they [told him what had happened].
sn46.53:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, when wanderers of other religions say this, you should say to them: | T: “Monks, when wanderers of other sects speak in that way, they should be addressed in this way:
sn46.53:2.2 | S: ‘Reverends, which awakening factors should not be developed when the mind is sluggish? And which awakening factors should be developed at that time? | T: ‘Friends, on any occasion when the mind is sluggish, which of the factors of awakening is that the wrong time to develop? Which of the factors of awakening is that the right time to develop?
sn46.53:2.3 | S: Which awakening factors should not be developed when the mind is restless? And which awakening factors should be developed at that time?’ | T: And on any occasion when the mind is restless, which of the factors of awakening is that the wrong time to develop? Which of the factors of awakening is that the right time to develop?’
sn46.53:2.4 | S: Questioned like this, the wanderers of other religions would be stumped, and, in addition, would get frustrated. | T: “Being asked in this way, the wanderers of other sects will be unable to respond and, on top of that, will fall into vexation.
sn46.53:2.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.53:2.6 | S: Because they’re out of their element. | T: Because it lies beyond their range.
sn46.53:3.1 | S: I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who could provide a satisfying answer to these questions except the Realized One or his disciple or someone who has heard it from them. | T: Monks, I don’t see anyone in this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, who would satisfy the mind with their answer to these questions, aside from the Tathāgata, a disciple of the Tathāgata, or one who had heard it from them.
sn46.53:4.1 | S: When the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: “Now, monks, on any occasion when the mind is sluggish, that is the wrong time to develop calm as a factor for awakening, concentration as a factor for awakening, equanimity as a factor for awakening.
sn46.53:4.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.53:4.3 | S: Because it’s hard to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: The sluggish mind is hard to raise up by those mental qualities.
sn46.53:5.1 | S: Suppose someone wanted to make a small fire flare up. | T: Just as if a man, wanting to make a small fire blaze up,
sn46.53:5.2 | S: If they toss wet grass, cow-dung, and timber on it, spray it with water, and scatter dirt on it, | T: were to place wet grass in it, wet cow dung, & wet sticks; were to give it a spray of water and smother it with dust.
sn46.53:5.3 | S: could they make it flare up?” | T: Is it possible that he would make the small fire blaze up?”
sn46.53:5.4 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn46.53:6.1 | S: “In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: “In the same way, monks, on any occasion the mind is sluggish, that is the wrong time to develop calm as a factor for awakening, concentration as a factor for awakening, equanimity as a factor for awakening.
sn46.53:6.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.53:6.3 | S: Because it’s hard to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: The sluggish mind is hard to raise up by those mental qualities.
sn46.53:7.1 | S: When the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture. | T: “Now, on any occasion when the mind is sluggish, that is the right time to develop analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, persistence as a factor for awakening, rapture as a factor for awakening.
sn46.53:7.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.53:7.3 | S: Because it’s easy to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: The sluggish mind is easy to raise up by those mental qualities.
sn46.53:8.1 | S: Suppose someone wanted to make a small fire flare up. | T: Just as if a man, wanting to make a small fire blaze up,
sn46.53:8.2 | S: If they toss dry grass, cow-dung, and timber on it, blow on it, and don’t scatter dirt on it, | T: were to place dry grass in it, dry cow dung, & dry sticks; were to blow on it with his mouth and not smother it with dust.
sn46.53:8.3 | S: could they make it flare up?” | T: Is it possible that he would make the small fire blaze up?
sn46.53:8.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.
sn46.53:9.1 | S: “In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture. | T: “In the same way, monks, on any occasion when the mind is sluggish, that is the right time to develop analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, persistence as a factor for awakening, rapture as a factor for awakening.
sn46.53:9.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.53:9.3 | S: Because it’s easy to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: The sluggish mind is easy to raise up by those mental qualities.
sn46.53:10.1 | S: When the mind is restless, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture. | T: “Now, on any occasion when the mind is restless, that is the wrong time to develop analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, persistence as a factor for awakening, rapture as a factor for awakening.
sn46.53:10.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?

