## sn46.54
sn46.54:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Koliyans, where they have a town named Haliddavasana. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Koliyans. Now there is a Koliyan town named Haliddavasana.
sn46.54:1.2 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Haliddavasana for alms. | T: Then, early in the morning, a large number of monks adjusted their lower robes and, taking their bowls & outer robes, went into Haliddavasana for alms.
sn46.54:1.3 | S: Then it occurred to them, | T: Then the thought occurred to them,
sn46.54:1.4 | S: “It’s too early to wander for alms in Haliddavasana. | T: “It’s still too early to go for alms in Haliddavasana.
sn46.54:1.5 | S: Why don’t we visit the monastery for the wanderers of other religions?” | T: Why don’t we go to the park of the wanderers of other sects?”
sn46.54:2.1 | S: Then they went to the monastery for the wanderers of other religions and exchanged greetings with the wanderers there. | T: So the monks went to the park of the wanderers of other sects. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with the wanderers of other sects.
sn46.54:2.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side. The wanderers said to them: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, the wanderers of other sects said to them,
sn46.54:3.1 | S: “Reverends, the ascetic Gotama teaches his disciples like this: | T: “Friends, Gotama the contemplative teaches the Dhamma to his disciples in this way:
sn46.54:3.2 | S: ‘Come, mendicants, give up these five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom, and meditate spreading a heart full of love to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, spread a heart full of love to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: ‘Come, monks—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—keep pervading the first direction [the east] with an awareness imbued with goodwill, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, keep pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with goodwill—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.
sn46.54:3.3 | S: Meditate spreading a heart full of compassion to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, spread a heart full of compassion to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with compassion.…
sn46.54:3.4 | S: Meditate spreading a heart full of rejoicing to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, spread a heart full of rejoicing to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with empathetic joy.…
sn46.54:3.5 | S: Meditate spreading a heart full of equanimity to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, they spread a heart full of equanimity to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will.’ | T: “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with equanimity, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, keep pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with equanimity—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.’
sn46.54:4.1 | S: We too teach our disciples in just the same way. | T: “Now, friends, we too teach our disciples in this way:
sn46.54:4.2 | P: ‘etha tumhe, āvuso, pañca nīvaraṇe pahāya cetaso upakkilese paññāya dubbalīkaraṇe mettāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha …pe… | T: ‘Come, you friends—abandoning the five hindrances, the corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment—keep pervading the first direction [the east] with an awareness imbued with goodwill, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, keep pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with goodwill—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.
sn46.54:4.3 | P: karuṇāsahagatena cetasā … | T: “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with compassion.…
sn46.54:4.4 | P: muditāsahagatena cetasā … | T: “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with empathetic joy.… “‘Keep pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with equanimity, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, keep pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with equanimity—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.’
sn46.54:4.5 | P: upekkhāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha, tathā dutiyaṁ, tathā tatiyaṁ, tathā catutthaṁ; | T: (none)
sn46.54:4.6 | P: iti uddhamadho tiriyaṁ sabbadhi sabbattatāya sabbāvantaṁ lokaṁ upekkhāsahagatena cetasā vipulena mahaggatena appamāṇena averena abyāpajjena pharitvā viharathā’ti. | T: (none)
sn46.54:4.7 | S: What, then, is the difference between the ascetic Gotama’s teaching and instruction and ours?” | T: “So, friends, what difference, what distinction, what distinguishing factor is there here between Gotama the contemplative and us, when comparing Dhamma teaching with Dhamma teaching, instruction with instruction?”
sn46.54:5.1 | S: Those mendicants neither approved nor rejected that statement of the wanderers of other religions. | T: Then the monks neither delighted in the words of the wanderers of other sects, nor did they reject them.
sn46.54:5.2 | S: They rose from their seats, thinking: | T: Without delighting or rejecting, they got up from their seats and left, (thinking,)
sn46.54:5.3 | S: “We will learn the meaning of this statement from the Buddha himself.” | T: “We will learn the meaning of these words in the presence of the Blessed One.”
sn46.54:5.4 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: So, having gone for alms in Haliddavasana, after the meal, returning from their alms round, the monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they [told him what had happened].
sn46.54:6.1 | P: “Idha mayaṁ, bhante, pubbaṇhasamayaṁ nivāsetvā pattacīvaramādāya haliddavasane piṇḍāya pavisimha. | T: (none)
sn46.54:6.2 | P: Tesaṁ no, bhante, amhākaṁ etadahosi: | T: (none)
sn46.54:6.3 | P: ‘atippago kho tāva haliddavasane piṇḍāya carituṁ. | T: (none)
sn46.54:7.1 | P: Yannūna mayaṁ yena aññatitthiyānaṁ paribbājakānaṁ ārāmo tenupasaṅkameyyāmā’ti. Atha kho mayaṁ, bhante, yena aññatitthiyānaṁ paribbājakānaṁ ārāmo tenupasaṅkamimha, upasaṅkamitvā tehi aññatitthiyehi paribbājakehi saddhiṁ sammodimha. | T: (none)
sn46.54:7.2 | P: Sammodanīyaṁ kathaṁ sāraṇīyaṁ vītisāretvā ekamantaṁ nisīdimha. Ekamantaṁ nisinne kho amhe, bhante, te aññatitthiyā paribbājakā etadavocuṁ: | T: (none)
sn46.54:8.1 | P: ‘Samaṇo, āvuso, gotamo sāvakānaṁ evaṁ dhammaṁ deseti: | T: (none)
sn46.54:8.2 | P: “etha tumhe, bhikkhave, pañca nīvaraṇe pahāya cetaso upakkilese paññāya dubbalīkaraṇe mettāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:8.3 | P: karuṇāsahagatena cetasā …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:8.4 | P: muditāsahagatena cetasā …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:8.5 | P: upekkhāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha, tathā dutiyaṁ, tathā tatiyaṁ, tathā catutthaṁ; iti uddhamadho tiriyaṁ sabbadhi sabbattatāya sabbāvantaṁ lokaṁ upekkhāsahagatena cetasā vipulena mahaggatena appamāṇena averena abyāpajjena pharitvā viharathā’ti. | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.1 | P: Mayampi kho, āvuso, sāvakānaṁ evaṁ dhammaṁ desema: | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.2 | P: ‘etha tumhe, āvuso, pañca nīvaraṇe pahāya cetaso upakkilese paññāya dubbalīkaraṇe mettāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.3 | P: karuṇāsahagatena cetasā …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.4 | P: muditāsahagatena cetasā …pe… | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.5 | P: upekkhāsahagatena cetasā ekaṁ disaṁ pharitvā viharatha, tathā dutiyaṁ, tathā tatiyaṁ, tathā catutthaṁ; iti uddhamadho tiriyaṁ sabbadhi sabbattatāya sabbāvantaṁ lokaṁ upekkhāsahagatena cetasā vipulena mahaggatena appamāṇena averena abyāpajjena pharitvā viharathā’ti. | T: (none)
sn46.54:9.6 | P: Idha no, āvuso, ko viseso, ko adhippayāso, kiṁ nānākaraṇaṁ samaṇassa vā gotamassa amhākaṁ vā, yadidaṁ, dhammadesanāya vā dhammadesanaṁ, anusāsaniyā vā anusāsaninti? | T: (none)
sn46.54:10.1 | P: Atha kho mayaṁ, bhante, tesaṁ aññatitthiyānaṁ paribbājakānaṁ bhāsitaṁ neva abhinandimha nappaṭikkosimha, anabhinanditvā appaṭikkositvā uṭṭhāyāsanā pakkamimha: | T: (none)
sn46.54:10.2 | P: ‘bhagavato santike etassa bhāsitassa atthaṁ ājānissāmā’”ti. | T: (none)
sn46.54:11.1 | S: “Mendicants, when wanderers of other religions say this, you should say to them: | T: “Monks, when wanderers of other sects speak in that way, they should be addressed in this way:
sn46.54:11.2 | S: ‘But reverends, how is the heart’s release by love developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and culmination? | T: ‘But how, friends, is awareness-release through goodwill developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?
sn46.54:11.3 | S: How is the heart’s release by compassion developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: How is awareness-release through compassion developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?
sn46.54:11.4 | S: How is the heart’s release by rejoicing developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: How is awareness-release through empathetic joy developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?
sn46.54:11.5 | S: How is the heart’s release by equanimity developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end?’ | T: How is awareness-release through equanimity developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?’
sn46.54:11.6 | S: Questioned like this, the wanderers of other religions would be stumped, and, in addition, would get frustrated. | T: “Being asked in this way, the wanderers of other sects will be unable to respond and, on top of that, will fall into vexation.
sn46.54:11.7 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn46.54:11.8 | S: Because they’re out of their element. | T: Because it lies beyond their range.
sn46.54:11.9 | S: I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who could provide a satisfying answer to these questions except the Realized One or his disciple or someone who has heard it from them. | T: Monks, I don’t see anyone in this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, who would satisfy the mind with their answer to these questions, aside from the Tathāgata, a disciple of the Tathāgata, or one who had heard it from them.
sn46.54:12.1 | S: And how is the heart’s release by love developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: “And how, monks, is awareness-release through goodwill developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?
sn46.54:12.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the heart’s release by love together with the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: “There is the case where a monk develops mindfulness as a factor for awakening accompanied by goodwill, dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go. He develops analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening… persistence as a factor for awakening… rapture as a factor for awakening… calm as a factor for awakening… concentration as a factor for awakening…
sn46.54:12.3 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: equanimity as a factor for awakening accompanied by goodwill, dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go.
sn46.54:12.4 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants, he remains percipient of loathsomeness in the presence of what is not loathsome.
sn46.54:12.5 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the unrepulsive in the repulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants, he remains percipient of unloathsomeness in the presence of what is loathsome.
sn46.54:12.6 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive and the repulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants, he remains percipient of loathsomeness in the presence of what is not loathsome & what is.
sn46.54:12.7 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the unrepulsive in the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants, he remains percipient of unloathsomeness in the presence of what is loathsome & what is not.
sn46.54:12.8 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate staying equanimous, mindful and aware, shunning both the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants—in the presence of what is loathsome & what is not—cutting himself off from both, he remains equanimous, alert, & mindful.
sn46.54:12.9 | S: The apex of the heart’s release by love is the beautiful, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom. | T: Or he may enter & remain in the beautiful liberation. I tell you, monks, awareness-release through goodwill has the beautiful as its excellence—in the case of one who has penetrated to no higher release.
sn46.54:13.1 | S: And how is the heart’s release by compassion developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: “And how is awareness-release through compassion developed, what is its destination, what is its excellence, its fruit, & its consummation?
sn46.54:13.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the heart’s release by compassion together with the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: “There is the case where a monk develops mindfulness as a factor for awakening accompanied by compassion, dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go. He develops analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening… persistence as a factor for awakening… rapture as a factor for awakening… calm as a factor for awakening… concentration as a factor for awakening…
sn46.54:13.3 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: equanimity as a factor for awakening accompanied by compassion, dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go.
sn46.54:13.4 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. … | T: If he wants, he remains percipient of loathsomeness in the presence of what is not loathsome. If he wants, he remains percipient of unloathsomeness in the presence of what is loathsome. If he wants, he remains percipient of loathsomeness in the presence of what is not loathsome & what is. If he wants, he remains percipient of unloathsomeness in the presence of what is loathsome & what is not.
sn46.54:13.5 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate staying equanimous, mindful and aware, shunning both the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wants—in the presence of what is loathsome & what is not—cutting himself off from both, he remains equanimous, alert, & mindful.
sn46.54:13.6 | S: Or else, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, they enter and remain in the dimension of infinite space. | T: Or, with the complete transcending of perceptions of (physical) form, with the disappearance of perceptions of resistance, and not attending to perceptions of multiplicity, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite space,’ he enters & remains in the sphere of the infinitude of space.
sn46.54:13.7 | S: The apex of the heart’s release by compassion is the dimension of infinite space, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom. | T: I tell you, monks, awareness-release through compassion has the sphere of the infinitude of space as its excellence—in the case of one who has penetrated to no higher release.
